## Flexbox (most common)

```css
.parent {
  width: 100vh;
  height: 100vh;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;  /* horizontal */
  align-items: center;      /* vertical */
}
```

## CSS Grid (shortest)

```css
.parent {
  width: 100vh;
  height: 100vh;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;      /* both axes in one line */
}
```

Both work with **any** child element (div, img, span, etc.) and require zero CSS on the child.

### Quick breakdown

| Property | Does what |
|---|---|
| `display: flex` / `grid` | Turns the parent into a layout container |
| `justify-content: center` | Centers along the **main** axis (horizontal by default) |
| `align-items: center` | Centers along the **cross** axis (vertical by default) |
| `place-items: center` | Grid shorthand for both of the above |

> **Tip:** If you ever need the flex direction to be vertical, swap the two: `align-items` handles horizontal and `justify-content` handles vertical. Grid's `place-items` is direction-agnostic, so it's the safest one-liner.

