# Fix: `printWindow.close()` Cancels the Print Dialog

The problem is a **race condition**: `print()` opens the print dialog asynchronously in most browsers, but `close()` executes immediately after and destroys the window (and its print dialog) before the user can interact with it.

## Solution 1 – Delay the `close()` with `setTimeout` (simplest & most reliable)

```javascript
var printWindow = window.open('', '_blank');

printWindow.document.open('text/plain');
printWindow.document.write("toto");
printWindow.document.close();
printWindow.focus();
printWindow.print();

// Give the browser time to render the print dialog before closing
setTimeout(function () {
    printWindow.close();
}, 1000);  // 1 second is usually enough; bump to 2000 if needed
```

> The exact delay may need tweaking per browser, but **1 000 ms** works in Chrome, Firefox, and Edge.

## Solution 2 – Close on `onafterprint` (cleaner, no magic number)

```javascript
var printWindow = window.open('', '_blank');

printWindow.document.open('text/plain');
printWindow.document.write("toto");
printWindow.document.close();
printWindow.focus();

// Close the window only AFTER the user confirms/cancels the print dialog
printWindow.onafterprint = function () {
    printWindow.close();
};

printWindow.print();
```

This is the "correct" approach because the window is closed **after** the print dialog is dismissed, regardless of how long the user takes.

> ⚠️ `onafterprint` is supported in modern browsers (Chrome 49+, Firefox 62+, Edge). If you must support very old browsers, fall back to Solution 1.

## Solution 3 – Don't close at all (safest fallback)

If you just need it to work everywhere with zero risk:

```javascript
printWindow.document.open('text/plain');
printWindow.document.write("toto");
printWindow.document.close();
printWindow.focus();
printWindow.print();
// Let the user close the blank tab manually
```

## Why it happens

| Call | What the browser does |
|---|---|
| `print()` | Queues the print dialog (asynchronous in most engines) |
| `close()` (immediately after) | Destroys the window **before** the dialog is fully rendered → dialog is cancelled / never shown |

By the time the browser gets to actually display the dialog, the window object no longer exists.

## Recommendation

Use **Solution 2** (`onafterprint`) if your user base is on modern browsers. Otherwise use **Solution 1** with a `setTimeout` of ~1 000 ms. Avoid Solution 3 in production if you can, since it leaves a stray blank tab.

