# Rust GUI File Watcher Application

Here's a complete Rust GUI application using **egui** (via `eframe`) for the interface, **notify** for file system monitoring, and **rfd** for native folder dialogs.

## Project Setup

Create a new project:

```bash
cargo create file_watcher_gui
cd file_watcher_gui
```

## `Cargo.toml`

```toml
[package]
name = "file_watcher_gui"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

[dependencies]
eframe = "0.29"
notify = "6"
rfd = "0.15"
```

## `src/main.rs`

```rust
use eframe::egui;
use notify::{Event, EventKind, RecommendedWatcher, RecursiveMode, Watcher};
use rfd::FileDialog;
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::sync::mpsc::{channel, Receiver};
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use std::time::SystemTime;

fn main() -> eframe::Result<()> {
    let options = eframe::NativeOptions {
        viewport: egui::ViewportBuilder::default()
            .with_inner_size([700.0, 500.0])
            .with_title("File Watcher"),
        ..Default::default()
    };

    eframe::run_native(
        "File Watcher",
        options,
        Box::new(|_cc| Ok(Box::new(FileWatcherApp::default()))),
    )
}

struct FileWatcherApp {
    input_folder: PathBuf,
    output_folder: PathBuf,
    done_folder: PathBuf,
    monitoring: bool,
    log: Vec<String>,
    watcher: Option<RecommendedWatcher>,
    receiver: Option<Receiver<Event>>,
    // Shared log for the watcher thread to push messages into
    shared_log: Arc<Mutex<Vec<String>>>,
}

impl Default for FileWatcherApp {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            input_folder: PathBuf::new(),
            output_folder: PathBuf::new(),
            done_folder: PathBuf::new(),
            monitoring: false,
            log: Vec::new(),
            watcher: None,
            receiver: None,
            shared_log: Arc::new(Mutex::new(Vec::new())),
        }
    }
}

impl FileWatcherApp {
    fn start_monitoring(&mut self) {
        let input_path = self.input_folder.clone();
        let (tx, rx) = channel::<Event>();
        self.receiver = Some(rx);

        // Clear previous log
        self.log.clear();
        self.shared_log.lock().unwrap().clear();

        let shared_log = Arc::clone(&self.shared_log);

        match RecommendedWatcher::new(
            move |result: Result<Event, notify::Error>| {
                match result {
                    Ok(event) => {
                        let msg = format_event(&event);
                        if let Ok(mut log) = shared_log.lock() {
                            log.push(msg);
                        }
                    }
                    Err(e) => {
                        if let Ok(mut log) = shared_log.lock() {
                            log.push(format!("[ERROR] Watcher error: {e}"));
                        }
                    }
                }
            },
            RecursiveMode::NonRecursive,
        ) {
            Ok(mut watcher) => {
                if let Err(e) = watcher.watch(&input_path, RecursiveMode::NonRecursive) {
                    let msg = format!("[ERROR] Failed to watch {input_path:?}: {e}");
                    self.log.push(msg);
                    self.shared_log.lock().unwrap().push(msg);
                    return;
                }
                self.log.push(format!(
                    "[INFO] Monitoring started: {}",
                    input_path.display()
                ));
                self.shared_log.lock().unwrap().push(format!(
                    "[INFO] Monitoring started: {}",
                    input_path.display()
                ));
                self.watcher = Some(watcher);
                self.monitoring = true;
            }
            Err(e) => {
                let msg = format!("[ERROR] Could not create watcher: {e}");
                self.log.push(msg);
                self.shared_log.lock().unwrap().push(msg);
            }
        }
    }

    fn stop_monitoring(&mut self) {
        self.watcher = None;
        self.receiver = None;
        self.monitoring = false;
        let msg = "[INFO] Monitoring stopped.".to_string();
        self.log.push(msg.clone());
        self.shared_log.lock().unwrap().push(msg);
    }

    /// Drain the receiver channel and merge new events into the log.
    fn poll_events(&mut self) {
        if let Some(ref rx) = self.receiver {
            // Drain all pending events
            loop {
                match rx.try_recv() {
                    Ok(event) => {
                        let msg = format_event(&event);
                        self.log.push(msg);
                    }
                    Err(std::sync::mpsc::TryRecvError::Empty) => break,
                    Err(std::sync::mpsc::TryRecvError::Disconnected) => {
                        let msg = "[ERROR] Watcher channel disconnected.".to_string();
                        self.log.push(msg);
                        break;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

impl eframe::App for FileWatcherApp {
    fn update(&mut self, ctx: &egui::Context, _frame: &mut eframe::Frame) {
        // Poll for new file events
        self.poll_events();

        egui::CentralPanel::default().show(ctx, |ui| {
            ui.heading("File Watcher");
            ui.separator();

            // --- Folder selection rows ---
            self.folder_row(ui, "Input", &mut self.input_folder);
            self.folder_row(ui, "Output", &mut self.output_folder);
            self.folder_row(ui, "Done", &mut self.done_folder);

            ui.separator();

            // --- Start / Stop button ---
            ui.horizontal(|ui| {
                if self.monitoring {
                    if ui.button("Stop").clicked() {
                        self.stop_monitoring();
                    }
                    ui.label(egui::RichText::new("● Monitoring…").color(egui::Color32::RED));
                } else {
                    let start_enabled = !self.input_folder.as_os_str().is_empty();
                    if ui.add_enabled(start_enabled, egui::Button::new("Start")).clicked() {
                        self.start_monitoring();
                    }
                    if !start_enabled {
                        ui.label(
                            egui::RichText::new("Select an input folder first.")
                                .color(egui::Color32::GRAY),
                        );
                    }
                }
            });

            ui.separator();

            // --- Log area ---
            ui.label("Event Log:");
            egui::ScrollArea::vertical()
                .auto_shrink([false, false])
                .show(ui, |ui| {
                    ui.set_min_height(ui.available_height() - 20.0);
                    if self.log.is_empty() {
                        ui.label(egui::RichText::new("(no events yet)").color(egui::Color32::GRAY));
                    }
                    for line in &self.log {
                        let color = if line.contains("[ERROR]") {
                            egui::Color32::RED
                        } else if line.contains("[INFO]") {
                            egui::Color32::from_rgb(100, 180, 255)
                        } else {
                            egui::Color32::LIGHT_GRAY
                        };
                        ui.label(egui::RichText::new(line).color(color).monospace());
                    }
                });
        });

        // Request repaint every 250 ms so the log updates even without user interaction
        ctx.request_repaint_after(Duration::from_millis(250));
    }
}

impl FileWatcherApp {
    fn folder_row(&mut self, ui: &mut egui::Ui, label: &str, path: &mut PathBuf) {
        ui.horizontal(|ui| {
            ui.label(egui::RichText::new(label).strong());
            if ui.button("Browse…").clicked() {
                if let Some(folder) = FileDialog::new().pick_folder() {
                    *path = folder;
                }
            }
            if path.as_os_str().is_empty() {
                ui.label(egui::RichText::new("(not selected)").color(egui::Color32::GRAY));
            } else {
                ui.label(egui::RichText::new(path.display().to_string()).monospace());
            }
        });
    }
}

/// Format a notify Event into a human-readable log line.
fn format_event(event: &Event) -> String {
    let timestamp = SystemTime::now()
        .duration_since(SystemTime::UNIX_EPOCH)
        .map(|d| d.as_secs())
        .unwrap_or(0);

    let kind_str = match &event.kind {
        EventKind::Create(_) => "CREATE",
        EventKind::Modify(_) => "MODIFY",
        EventKind::Remove(_) => "REMOVE",
        EventKind::Access(_) => "ACCESS",
        EventKind::MetadataChange(_) => "METADATA",
        EventKind::Other => "OTHER",
        _ => "UNKNOWN",
    };

    let paths: Vec<String> = event
        .paths
        .iter()
        .map(|p| p.display().to_string())
        .collect();

    format!(
        "[{timestamp}] {kind_str:>10} | {}",
        paths.join(", ")
    )
}
```

## How It Works

| Component | Role |
|-----------|------|
| **`eframe` / `egui`** | Provides the window and immediate-mode GUI (buttons, labels, scroll area). |
| **`rfd`** | Opens the OS-native folder-picker dialog when "Browse…" is clicked. |
| **`notify`** | Watches the input directory via the OS file-system API (inotify on Linux, FSEvents on macOS, ReadDirectoryChangesW on Windows). |
| **`mpsc` channel** | The `notify` watcher pushes `Event`s into a channel; the GUI thread drains it each frame and appends formatted lines to the log. |

## Running

```bash
cargo run
```

A window appears with three **Browse…** buttons (Input / Output / Done), a **Start** button, and a scrollable event log. After you pick an input folder and click **Start**, any file created, modified, or deleted inside that folder produces a timestamped line like:

```
[1717000000]   CREATE | /home/user/incoming/report.pdf
[1717000002]   MODIFY | /home/user/incoming/report.pdf
```

The **Stop** button tears down the watcher and resets the log.

> **Note:** The `output` and `done` folders are captured for your future processing logic (e.g., moving processed files). The current build only *monitors* the input folder as requested; you can extend `start_monitoring` to spawn a worker thread that moves files from input → output → done.

{
