  <h1>The Last Romans: A Question of Continuity</h1>

  <div class="subtitle">How a two-thousand-year-old linguistic fact became the load-bearing wall of a national identity, and why nobody quite agrees on what it means.</div>

  <div class="byline-block">
    <span class="author">By the Editorial Desk</span>
    <span class="date">June 2025</span>
  </div>

  <div class="article-body">

    <p>Romania is the odd one out in Eastern Europe. While its neighbors speak Slavic languages, the people of Wallachia, Moldavia, and Transylvania speak a language that is, at its root, the Latin of a Roman soldier's camp. This fact has generated more heat per square kilometer of Carpathian forest than perhaps any other question in European ethnography. The core question is deceptively simple: when the Roman legions pulled out of Dacia in the third century, did a Latin-speaking population stay behind, hunker down in the mountains, and wait two thousand years for the rest of the continent to catch up? Or did they vanish, to be replaced by a later wave of Romance speakers arriving from the Balkans or the Adriatic? The answer, it turns out, is not merely an academic curiosity. It is the load-bearing wall of a national identity, and for that reason it has been fought over with a ferocity that would make a medieval genealogist weep.</p>

    <p>The continuity hypothesis, known in Romanian scholarship as the Daco-Romanian theory, holds that the conquest of Dacia by Trajan in 106 AD set in motion a process of Romanization that outlasted the empire itself. Roman administrators, veterans, and traders intermarried with the local Dacian population, and a creolized Latin dialect took root in the valleys and highlands. When the Gothic invasions, the Huns, and the Slavic migrations swept through the Balkans in the centuries that followed, a remnant of this population retreated into the Carpathian mountains, preserving their language in the way that a small community preserves a dialect when the world around it changes. Proponents point to the continuity of certain place names, the absence of a large-scale Slavic replacement of the population, and the fact that Romanian's core vocabulary is overwhelmingly Latin. The theory was codified in the nineteenth century by historians like Gheorghe Asachi and later refined by a generation of Romanian philologists who treated it less as a hypothesis to be tested and more as a truth to be defended. It remains the official position of Romanian historiography, and the one most Romanians would tell you over a glass of țuică at a family dinner.</p>

    <p>The immigrationist school, by contrast, argues that the Roman population of Dacia was either destroyed, expelled, or thoroughly assimilated during the violent centuries of the Great Migrations, and that the Romance-speaking communities which begin to appear in Byzantine and medieval records are, in a meaningful sense, newcomers. These later arrivals, the argument goes, could have been Greek-speaking Byzantine populations who shifted to a Romance dialect, Italian and French merchants who settled in the Danubian principalities, or even small groups of Romance speakers from the Balkan peninsula who migrated northward in the early medieval period. The most frequently cited linguistic evidence is the depth of the Slavic layer in Romanian—far more extensive than in Italian, French, or Spanish—which immigrationists read as a sign that the language was transplanted into a Slavic-speaking environment rather than having developed in situ alongside it. This school has never held the mainstream in Romanian academia, but it has found sympathetic ears among some Hungarian and German scholars, and it periodically resurfaces in the popular press with a vigor that suggests it is doing more work as a political argument than as a philological one. The question, in other words, is less about whether any Latin speakers were present in the region in the third and fourth centuries—almost certainly they were—and more about whether that population constituted a continuous, self-reproducing community or a small, fragile residue that was repeatedly supplemented by new arrivals.</p>

    <p>Most working linguists and historians, when they are not writing for a national audience, occupy a more comfortable middle ground. There was almost certainly a Latin-speaking population in the region after the Roman withdrawal, but it was small, rural, and concentrated in the mountainous interior—exactly the kind of community that would be hardest to document in the sparse medieval record. The Slavic influence is real and significant, but its presence does not automatically imply a total population replacement; languages borrow from their neighbors, and a small Romance community surrounded by Slavic speakers would naturally absorb a great deal of Slavic vocabulary. The toponymic evidence is suggestive but not conclusive, and the archaeological record for the period between the fifth and ninth centuries is, to put it gently, thin. What the middle-ground position concedes is that "continuity" is not the same as "stasis": the community that produced Romanian was not a sealed jar of Trajanic Latin, but a living, borrowing, adapting population shaped by every group that passed through the Carpathian basin. The debate, in the end, may be less about whether the answer is A or B and more about the degree to which the community was continuous, how large it was, and how much it changed along the way.</p>

    <p>The political charge of the question should not be underestimated, and it is not merely a Romanian affair. In the nineteenth century, as the Romanian principalities were consolidating into a nation-state and arguing for recognition at the Congress of Berlin, the Daco-Romanian theory provided something invaluable: a pedigree that was not Slavic, not Hungarian, not Turkic, but Latin. It made the Romanians, in the eyes of Western European diplomats, less like the "Orient" and more like a distant cousin of the French and Italians, a people who had simply been stuck in the mountains a bit too long. The theory was, in this context, a diplomatic weapon, and it was wielded with considerable skill. Under the Communists, the narrative was repurposed again, this time to emphasize the "progressive" role of the Roman state in bringing civilization to the "barbarian" Dacians, a framing that conveniently elided the violence of conquest and the subsequent centuries of Ottoman and Habsburg domination. In the Hungarian context, the question takes on a different valence: if the Romanians' presence in Transylvania is the product of a continuous two-thousand-year settlement, it complicates the Hungarian claim to the region as a historical and legal matter. The result is that a question about the phonology of a mountain dialect has become, in the popular imagination, a question about who owns a piece of land, and no amount of peer-reviewed scholarship has fully defused that particular fuse.</p>

    <p>Where does that leave us? The academic consensus, such as it is, leans toward a modified continuity: a Latin-speaking population persisted in the Carpathian region after the Roman withdrawal, but it was small, it was shaped profoundly by Slavic and other contacts, and the medieval and early modern periods saw additional Romance-speaking immigration that reinforced and reshaped the community. The Daco-Romanian theory, in its most rigid nineteenth-century formulation, is probably too neat; the immigrationist theory, in its most extreme form, is probably too dismissive. What is harder to resolve is the emotional and political weight that has accumulated around the question, the way that a two-thousand-year-old linguistic fact has been pressed into service as a founding myth, a diplomatic argument, and a territorial claim. The Romanians of today are, in the end, the product of every one of those processes—Roman, Slavic, Hungarian, Turkic, Byzantine, and their own particular genius for making something new out of all of it. And perhaps that is the most Roman thing about them: the ability to take the rubble of an empire and build, out of it, a language that still sounds, when spoken quickly in a mountain village, like the ghost of a legionnaire's Latin.</p>

