# AudioCapture in a GameMode (UE5, C++)

Below is a complete, working example. It uses the standalone `UAudioCapture` object (a plain `UObject`, not a component) owned by your `AGameModeBase` subclass.

## 1. Module dependency

In your game module's `.Build.cs`, make sure the **AudioMixer** module is listed:

```cpp
// MyProject.Build.cs
PublicDependencyModuleNames.AddRange(new string[] {
    "Core",
    "CoreUObject",
    "Engine",
    "AudioMixer",          // ← provides UAudioCapture
});
```

## 2. Header – `MyGameMode.h`

```cpp
#pragma once

#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "GameFramework/GameModeBase.h"
#include "AudioCapture.h"          // UAudioCapture + FOnAudioDataCaptured
#include "MyGameMode.generated.h"

UCLASS()
class MYPROJECT_API AMyGameMode : public AGameModeBase
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    AMyGameMode();

    // GameMode lifecycle
    virtual void InitGame(const FString& MapName, const FString& Options, FString& ErrorMessage) override;
    virtual void StartPlay() override;
    virtual void EndPlay(const EEndPlayReason::Type EndPlayReason) override;

private:
    // Owns the capture object for the lifetime of the GameMode.
    UPROPERTY()
    TObjectPtr<UAudioCapture> AudioCapture;

    // Delegate handler – receives raw PCM samples every capture tick.
    void OnAudioDataCaptured(const TArray<uint8>& AudioData, uint32 NumSamples);
};
```

## 3. Source – `MyGameMode.cpp`

```cpp
#include "MyGameMode.h"
#include "AudioCapture.h"
#include "Misc/ScopeLock.h"
#include "HAL/CriticalSection.h"

AMyGameMode::AMyGameMode()
{
    // Nothing special needed in the constructor.
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Called once when the game is initialised (before any actors spawn).
//  We create the UAudioCapture here so it exists for the whole session.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
void AMyGameMode::InitGame(const FString& MapName, const FString& Options, FString& ErrorMessage)
{
    Super::InitGame(MapName, Options, ErrorMessage);

    // Create the capture object.  It is a plain UObject – no component needed.
    AudioCapture = NewObject<UAudioCapture>(this);

    // Bind our handler to the delegate that fires on the game thread
    // every time a new buffer of samples is ready.
    AudioCapture->OnAudioDataCaptured.AddDynamic(this, &AMyGameMode::OnAudioDataCaptured);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Called when the game actually starts playing (after the level is loaded
//  and the first frame is about to render).  This is the right place to
//  request microphone access and begin the capture loop.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
void AMyGameMode::StartPlay()
{
    Super::StartPlay();

    if (AudioCapture && !AudioCapture->IsCapturing())
    {
        AudioCapture->StartCapture();
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Log, TEXT("AudioCapture started."));
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Called when the game session ends (level change, quit, etc.).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
void AMyGameMode::EndPlay(const EEndPlayReason::Type EndPlayReason)
{
    if (AudioCapture && AudioCapture->IsCapturing())
    {
        AudioCapture->OnAudioDataCaptured.RemoveDynamic(this, &AMyGameMode::OnAudioDataCaptured);
        AudioCapture->StopCapture();
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Log, TEXT("AudioCapture stopped."));
    }

    Super::EndPlay(EndPlayReason);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Called on the GAME THREAD every time a new chunk of audio is captured.
//  AudioData  – interleaved 16-bit little-endian PCM (mono or stereo).
//  NumSamples – number of *frames* (not bytes) in the buffer.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
void AMyGameMode::OnAudioDataCaptured(const TArray<uint8>& AudioData, uint32 NumSamples)
{
    // Example: compute a simple RMS level and log it.
    // Adjust for your actual channel count (1 = mono, 2 = stereo).
    constexpr int32 NumChannels = 1;
    constexpr int32 BytesPerSample = 2; // 16-bit PCM

    if (NumSamples == 0 || AudioData.Num() == 0)
    {
        return;
    }

    const int16* Samples = reinterpret_cast<const int16*>(AudioData.GetData());
    double SumSquares = 0.0;
    const int32 TotalSamples = static_cast<int32>(NumSamples) * NumChannels;

    for (int32 i = 0; i < TotalSamples; ++i)
    {
        const double S = static_cast<double>(Samples[i]);
        SumSquares += S * S;
    }

    const double RMS = FMath::Sqrt(SumSquares / static_cast<double>(TotalSamples));
    const float NormalisedLevel = static_cast<float>(RMS / 32768.0); // 0..1

    UE_LOG(LogTemp, Log, TEXT("Audio level (RMS): %.4f  (%u frames)"),
           NormalisedLevel, NumSamples);

    // ---- Your real logic goes here ----
    // e.g. feed into a voice-chat encoder, do a sound-reactive effect,
    // store in a ring buffer for later analysis, etc.
}
```

## 4. Key points / gotchas

| Topic | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Why `InitGame` vs `StartPlay`?** | `InitGame` runs before the level is fully ready – good for *creating* the object. `StartPlay` runs when play actually begins – the right moment to call `StartCapture()` so the OS mic prompt (if any) appears at the right time. |
| **Thread safety** | The `OnAudioDataCaptured` delegate fires on the **game thread**, so you don't need extra locking for typical game logic. If you push data into a background worker, add your own `FCriticalSection` / `FCriticalSection` guard. |
| **Format** | By default the buffer is **16-bit signed little-endian PCM**, interleaved. The number of channels is whatever the OS default input device provides (usually 1 or 2). If you need a specific format, you can set `UAudioCapture::NumChannels` / `UAudioCapture::SampleRate` before calling `StartCapture()`. |
| **Lifetime** | Because `AudioCapture` is a `UPROPERTY` on the GameMode (an Actor → UObject), the GC will keep it alive as long as the GameMode is alive. You never need to `DeleteObject` it manually. |
| **Platform notes** | On **Windows** the first `StartCapture()` call will trigger the OS microphone permission prompt. On **iOS / Android** you must also declare the appropriate Info.plist / AndroidManifest permissions (`NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` / `RECORD_AUDIO`). |
| **Not a component** | Because this is a bare `UAudioCapture` (not `UAudioCaptureComponent`), it is **not** attached to any actor in the scene, has no transform, and won't appear in the Outliner. It lives purely in memory owned by the GameMode. |

This gives you a self-contained, GameMode-scoped microphone capture that starts when the game starts and tears down cleanly when it ends.

# The Problem: `startMarker` Is the Same Object You're Moving

Your main bug is this line in `moveToCenter()`:

```csharp
startMarker = this.transform;
```

You're assigning the **same transform** you're animating. So every frame inside the coroutine, `startMarker.position` is the object's *current* (already-moving) position, not where it started. Your Lerp effectively becomes:

```csharp
transform.position = Vector3.Lerp(transform.position, endPos, journeyAmount);
```

This means the "start" keeps sliding under the object, the motion is non-linear, and `endPos` appears to never be reached cleanly (or the object overshoots/oscillates depending on timing).

## The Fix

Store the starting **position** as a `Vector3`, not a reference to the live transform:

```csharp
public class moveCard : MonoBehaviour
{
    public storage Storage;
    public float speed = 20f;
    private float startTime;
    private float journeyLength;
    private Vector3 startPos;       // <-- store a snapshot of the position
    private Vector3 startRotation;  // <-- same idea for rotation
    private float journeyAmount;
    private float distance;
    public int dir = 1;
    private Vector3 endPos;
    private Coroutine moveCoroutine; // <-- keep a reference to the running coroutine

    void Awake()
    {
        if (gameObject.name == "reference")
        {
            Storage.movePos = transform.position;
        }
    }

    public void moveToCenter()
    {
        startPos = transform.position;   // snapshot
        startRotation = transform.rotation;
        startTime = Time.time;

        endPos = new Vector3(Storage.movePos.x * dir, Storage.movePos.y, Storage.movePos.z);
        journeyLength = Vector3.Distance(transform.position, endPos);

        moveCoroutine = StartCoroutine(move());
        Invoke("stopAnimation", 2f);
    }

    IEnumerator move()
    {
        while (true)
        {
            distance = (Time.time - startTime) * speed;
            journeyAmount = distance / journeyLength;   // <-- calculate AFTER distance is set

            if (journeyAmount < 1f)
            {
                transform.position = Vector3.Lerp(startPos, endPos, journeyAmount);
                transform.rotation = Quaternion.Lerp(startRotation, Quaternion.Euler(Storage.rot), journeyAmount);
                yield return null;
            }
            else
            {
                // snap to exact end values
                transform.position = endPos;
                transform.rotation = Quaternion.Euler(Storage.rot);
                yield break;
            }
        }
    }

    private void stopAnimation()
    {
        if (moveCoroutine != null)
        {
            StopCoroutine(moveCoroutine);  // <-- stop the ACTUAL running coroutine
            moveCoroutine = null;
        }
    }
}
```

## Summary of what was wrong

| # | Bug | Why it matters |
|---|-----|----------------|
| 1 | `startMarker = this.transform` | The "start" moves with the object every frame, so the Lerp never behaves linearly and `endPos` is effectively chased but never cleanly reached. |
| 2 | `journeyAmount = distance / journeyLength` computed **before** `distance` is updated | First frame uses `distance = 0`; every frame is one tick behind. |
| 3 | `StopCoroutine(move())` in `stopAnimation` | Calling `move()` creates a **new** IEnumerator that was never started. You need to stop the one you actually started. |
| 4 | No final snap to `endPos` | Floating-point Lerp at `journeyAmount ≈ 1` can leave a tiny offset. Explicitly set the end values in the `else` branch. |

The core takeaway: **never hold a `Transform` reference as your "start" when you're moving that same transform.** Snapshot the `Vector3` (and `Quaternion`) at the moment the animation begins.

