# Routing Spring Security Filters by `Referer` Header

Spring Security's filter chains are normally matched by URL pattern, but you can use a **custom `RequestMatcher`** combined with **multiple `SecurityFilterChain` beans** to dispatch on any header, including `Referer`.

## 1. The Custom RequestMatcher

```java
public class RefererRequestMatcher implements RequestMatcher {

    private final String refererPrefix;

    public RefererRequestMatcher(String refererPrefix) {
        this.refererPrefix = refererPrefix;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean matches(HttpServletRequest request) {
        String referer = request.getHeader("Referer");
        return referer != null && referer.startsWith(refererPrefix);
    }
}
```

You can make the matching logic as strict or loose as you need (exact match, regex, list of allowed origins, etc.).

## 2. Multiple `SecurityFilterChain` Beans

Spring Security evaluates the beans **in `@Order` sequence** and applies the **first** chain whose matcher matches.

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    // ---- Chain for requests coming from app1 ----
    @Bean
    @Order(1)
    public SecurityFilterChain app1Chain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .securityMatcher(new RefererRequestMatcher("https://app1.example.com"))
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/api/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .oauth2Login(oauth2 -> oauth2
                .loginPage("/login/app1")
                // ... app1-specific OAuth2 / form-login config
            );
        return http.build();
    }

    // ---- Chain for requests coming from app2 ----
    @Bean
    @Order(2)
    public SecurityFilterChain app2Chain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .securityMatcher(new RefererRequestMatcher("https://app2.example.com"))
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/api/admin/**").hasAnyRole("ADMIN", "MANAGER")
                .anyRequest().permitAll()
            )
            .formLogin(form -> form
                .loginPage("/login/app2")
                // ... app2-specific config
            );
        return http.build();
    }

    // ---- Default / fallback chain ----
    @Bean
    @Order(3)
    public SecurityFilterChain defaultChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .anyRequest().denyAll()
            );
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

## 3. How It Works at Runtime

```
Request arrives
       │
       ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────┐
│  app1Chain (Order 1)           │  Referer starts with
│  securityMatcher: Referer =    │  "https://app1.example.com"?
│  app1.example.com              │  ──► YES → use this chain
└─────────────────────────────────┘
       │ NO
       ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────┐
│  app2Chain (Order 2)           │  Referer starts with
│  securityMatcher: Referer =    │  "https://app2.example.com"?
│  app2.example.com              │  ──► YES → use this chain
└─────────────────────────────────┘
       │ NO
       ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────┐
│  defaultChain (Order 3)        │  Catch-all
└─────────────────────────────────┘
```

## 4. If You Need More Than Just a Prefix

You can swap the matching logic for anything:

```java
// Exact match
return referer.equals(this.exactValue);

// List of allowed origins
private final Set<String> allowedOrigins;
return allowedOrigins.stream()
    .anyMatch(origin -> referer.startsWith(origin));

// Regex
private final Pattern pattern;
return pattern.matcher(referer).find();
```

## 5. ⚠️ Important Caveats

| Concern | Detail |
|---|---|
| **`Referer` is client-controlled** | Any client can set or omit the header. **Do not use it as a security boundary.** If an attacker omits the header, your request falls through to the default chain. |
| **`Referer` may be stripped** | Browsers drop it on HTTPS→HTTP transitions, and some privacy settings remove it entirely. Your "default chain" must handle this gracefully. |
| **Consider `Origin` or `Host`** | If your real goal is to distinguish which *frontend app* is calling, a custom header (e.g. `X-App-Id`) set by a reverse proxy / API gateway is far more reliable. |
| **CSRF** | If you're using form login or session-based auth, make sure CSRF tokens are scoped correctly per chain. |

## 6. Alternative: Single Chain + Custom Filter

If the differences between your "chains" are small (e.g. just a different set of roles), it can be simpler to keep **one** `SecurityFilterChain` and inject a custom `OncePerRequestFilter` that reads the `Referer` and adjusts the `SecurityContext` or delegates to different logic:

```java
@Component
@Order(1) // before UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter
public class RefererRoutingFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest req,
                                    HttpServletResponse res,
                                    FilterChain chain)
            throws ServletException, IOException {

        String referer = req.getHeader("Referer");
        // e.g. stash a flag in the request attribute that a later
        // AuthorizationManager or filter can inspect
        req.setAttribute("routing.app", resolveApp(referer));

        chain.doFilter(req, res);
    }

    private String resolveApp(String referer) {
        if (referer != null && referer.startsWith("https://app1.example.com")) return "app1";
        if (referer != null && referer.startsWith("https://app2.example.com")) return "app2";
        return "default";
    }
}
```

Then in your single chain you can use a custom `AuthorizationManager` that reads `request.getAttribute("routing.app")` to apply different rules.

---

**TL;DR** — For genuinely different filter chains (different login mechanisms, different token decoders, etc.), go with the **multiple `SecurityFilterChain` + custom `RequestMatcher`** approach. For small policy differences, a **single chain + custom filter** is less boilerplate. And in both cases, treat `Referer` as a *routing hint*, not a *trust signal*.

