# Automatic Garbage Collection for Linked Lists in C++

Here's a **mark-and-sweep** garbage collector that automatically reclaims unreachable linked-list nodes. It handles cycles (unlike `shared_ptr`) and triggers collection after a configurable number of allocations.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <set>
#include <vector>
#include <functional>

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Node: a linked-list node the GC is aware of
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
struct Node {
    int   data;
    Node* next;
    bool  marked;   // used during the mark phase

    Node(int val) : data(val), next(nullptr), marked(false) {
        GC::registerNode(this);
    }

    ~Node() {
        GC::unregisterNode(this);
    }
};

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//  GC: mark-and-sweep collector
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
class GC {
public:
    // Register a root (e.g. a list head) the GC should keep alive
    static void addRoot(Node* root)   { roots_.insert(root); }
    static void removeRoot(Node* root){ roots_.erase(root); }

    // Force a collection cycle
    static void collect() {
        mark();
        sweep();
        std::cout << "  [GC] collected. "
                  << allNodes_.size() << " node(s) remain.\n";
    }

    // Called automatically after every allocation
    static void maybeCollect() {
        ++allocCount_;
        if (allocCount_ >= threshold_) {
            collect();
            allocCount_ = 0;
        }
    }

    static void setThreshold(int n) { threshold_ = n; }
    static int  liveCount()         { return static_cast<int>(allNodes_.size()); }

    // ── internal ──
    static void registerNode(Node* n)   { allNodes_.insert(n); maybeCollect(); }
    static void unregisterNode(Node* n) { allNodes_.erase(n); }

private:
    static std::set<Node*> allNodes_;   // every node ever allocated (not yet swept)
    static std::set<Node*> roots_;      // user-designated roots (list heads, etc.)
    static int             allocCount_;
    static int             threshold_;

    // Mark phase: BFS/DFS from every root, set marked = true
    static void mark() {
        // Reset all flags
        for (auto* n : allNodes_) n->marked = false;

        // BFS from each root
        std::vector<Node*> stack;
        for (auto* r : roots_) {
            if (r && !r->marked) stack.push_back(r);
        }
        while (!stack.empty()) {
            Node* cur = stack.back();
            stack.pop_back();
            if (cur->marked) continue;
            cur->marked = true;
            if (cur->next) stack.push_back(cur->next);
            // For a more complex structure you'd walk every pointer field here
        }
    }

    // Sweep phase: delete every unmarked node
    static void sweep() {
        std::vector<Node*> toDelete;
        for (auto* n : allNodes_) {
            if (!n->marked) toDelete.push_back(n);
        }
        for (auto* n : toDelete) {
            std::cout << "  [GC] freeing node data=" << n->data << "\n";
            delete n;   // destructor calls unregisterNode → removes from allNodes_
        }
    }
};

// Static member definitions
std::set<Node*> GC::allNodes_;
std::set<Node*> GC::roots_;
int             GC::allocCount_ = 0;
int             GC::threshold_  = 5;   // collect every 5 allocations

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Helper: build a simple linked list
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
Node* makeList(std::initializer_list<int> vals) {
    Node* head = nullptr;
    for (int v : vals) {
        Node* n = new Node(v);
        n->next = head;
        head = n;
    }
    return head;
}

void printList(const char* label, Node* head) {
    std::cout << label << " → ";
    for (Node* p = head; p; p = p->next)
        std::cout << p->data << " → ";
    std::cout << "∅   (live nodes: " << GC::liveCount() << ")\n";
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Demo
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
int main() {
    std::cout << "=== Linked-List Garbage Collector Demo ===\n\n";

    // -- 1. Create two lists, register them as roots --
    Node* listA = makeList({10, 20, 30});
    Node* listB = makeList({100, 200, 300, 400});

    GC::addRoot(listA);
    GC::addRoot(listB);

    printList("List A", listA);
    printList("List B", listB);

    // -- 2. Orphan list B (remove root) → its nodes become garbage --
    std::cout << "\n-- Dropping root for List B (orphaning it) --\n";
    GC::removeRoot(listB);
    listB = nullptr;          // we no longer hold a pointer

    // -- 3. Force a collection --
    std::cout << "-- Running GC.collect() --\n";
    GC::collect();

    printList("List A", listA);

    // -- 4. Create more lists to show auto-collection threshold --
    std::cout << "\n-- Allocating more lists (auto-collect every "
              << GC::threshold_ << " allocs) --\n";

    Node* listC = makeList({5, 15, 25});
    GC::addRoot(listC);
    printList("List C", listC);

    // Orphan list A
    std::cout << "\n-- Dropping root for List A --\n";
    GC::removeRoot(listA);
    listA = nullptr;

    // Allocate enough to trigger auto-collect
    Node* listD = makeList({999});
    GC::addRoot(listD);
    printList("List D", listD);

    // -- 5. Final cleanup --
    std::cout << "\n-- Final GC.collect() --\n";
    GC::removeRoot(listC);
    GC::removeRoot(listD);
    GC::collect();

    std::cout << "\nAll done. Live nodes: " << GC::liveCount() << "\n";
    return 0;
}
```

## How It Works

```
  Allocation                    Mark phase                    Sweep phase
  ──────────                    ──────────                    ──────────

  new Node(10) ──► registry    root ──► 10 ──► 20 ──► 30     unmarked
  new Node(20)                  (marked)  (marked)  (marked)  nodes → delete
  new Node(30)                  root ──► 100 (marked)         marked
                               root ──► 777 (marked)          nodes → keep
```

| Phase | What happens |
|---|---|
| **Register** | Every `new Node(...)` inserts itself into `allNodes_` and increments a counter. |
| **Mark** | Starting from every **root** (list head you registered), walk `next` pointers and set `marked = true`. |
| **Sweep** | Iterate `allNodes_`; any node with `marked == false` is `delete`d. |
| **Auto-trigger** | After `threshold_` allocations, `collect()` runs automatically. |

## Key Design Choices

- **`std::set<Node*>`** for the registry → O(log n) insert/erase, no duplicates.
- **Iterative BFS** in `mark()` → no stack overflow on very long lists.
- **Roots are user-managed** (`addRoot` / `removeRoot`) → you explicitly say "this head pointer is a liveness anchor."
- **Cycles are safe** — unlike `shared_ptr`, a cycle `A→B→A` with no external root will be collected because neither node is reachable from any root.

## Extending It

- **More pointer fields** (e.g., a doubly-linked list with `prev`): add `if (cur->prev) stack.push_back(cur->prev);` in `mark()`.
- **Thread safety**: wrap `allNodes_` / `roots_` in a `std::mutex`.
- **Generation / incremental GC**: split `allNodes_` into young/old generations and only mark the young set each cycle.
- **Replace the threshold** with a time-based trigger (`std::chrono`) for a more realistic "background" collector.

