# Strategy + Registry Pattern (Open/Closed)

The best fit here is a combination of the **Strategy Pattern** (each account type is a strategy for "how to fetch my entity") and the **Registry Pattern** (a central lookup that knows all strategies). This gives you the **Open/Closed Principle**: adding a new account type requires *zero* changes to existing code.

## Architecture Overview

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                   AccountService                        │
│         (facade – the only thing callers use)           │
└────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┘
                         │ delegates to
                         ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│           AccountProviderRegistry  (Registry)           │
│   Map<AccountType, AccountProvider>                     │
└───────┬──────────────────┬──────────────────┬───────────┘
        │                  │                  │
        ▼                  ▼                  ▼
┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐
│ DoctorProvider│  │ UserProvider │  │ FutureProvider│  ← just add a new class
│ (Strategy)   │  │ (Strategy)   │  │ (Strategy)   │
└──────┬───────┘  └──────┬───────┘  └──────┬───────┘
       │                  │                  │
       ▼                  ▼                  ▼
  DoctorRepo          UserRepo          FutureRepo
```

## Full Code

### 1. Common Interface (the "contract")

```java
public interface Account {
    Integer getId();
    String getPhone();
    String getPasswordHash();
    String getFullName();
}
```

### 2. Entities implement the interface

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "doctor_account")
public class DoctorAccount implements Account {

    @Id
    private Integer id;

    private Integer specialtyId;

    @Column(unique = true)
    private String phone;

    private String passwordHash;

    private String fullName;

    // getters / setters
    @Override public Integer getId() { return id; }
    @Override public String getPhone() { return phone; }
    @Override public String getPasswordHash() { return passwordHash; }
    @Override public String getFullName() { return fullName; }
    // ...
}
```

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "user_account")
public class UserAccount implements Account {

    @Id
    private Integer id;

    @Column(unique = true)
    private String phone;

    private String passwordHash;

    private String fullName;

    // getters / setters
    @Override public Integer getId() { return id; }
    @Override public String getPhone() { return phone; }
    @Override public String getPasswordHash() { return passwordHash; }
    @Override public String getFullName() { return fullName; }
    // ...
}
```

### 3. Common Repository Interface

```java
public interface AccountRepository<T extends Account> extends JpaRepository<T, Integer> {
    // shared query methods can go here, e.g.:
    // Optional<T> findByPhone(String phone);
}
```

```java
public interface DoctorAccountRepository extends AccountRepository<DoctorAccount> { }

public interface UserAccountRepository extends AccountRepository<UserAccount> { }
```

### 4. Account Type Enum

```java
public enum AccountType {
    DOCTOR,
    USER
    // FUTURE: ADMIN, PATIENT, PHARMACIST …
}
```

### 5. Strategy Interface – one per account type

```java
public interface AccountProvider {

    /** Which type does this provider handle? */
    AccountType getType();

    /** Fetch by id; return null if not found. */
    Account findById(Integer id);
}
```

### 6. Concrete Strategies (one small class per type)

```java
@Component
public class DoctorAccountProvider implements AccountProvider {

    private final DoctorAccountRepository repository;

    public DoctorAccountProvider(DoctorAccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Override
    public AccountType getType() {
        return AccountType.DOCTOR;
    }

    @Override
    public Account findById(Integer id) {
        return repository.findById(id).orElse(null);
    }
}
```

```java
@Component
public class UserAccountProvider implements AccountProvider {

    private final UserAccountRepository repository;

    public UserAccountProvider(UserAccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Override
    public AccountType getType() {
        return AccountType.USER;
    }

    @Override
    public Account findById(Integer id) {
        return repository.findById(id).orElse(null);
    }
}
```

### 7. The Registry (auto-collects every strategy via Spring DI)

```java
@Component
public class AccountProviderRegistry {

    private final Map<AccountType, AccountProvider> providers;

    /**
     * Spring injects ALL beans that implement AccountProvider.
     * When you add a new provider, it appears here automatically.
     */
    public AccountProviderRegistry(List<AccountProvider> allProviders) {
        this.providers = allProviders.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.toMap(
                        AccountProvider::getType,
                        Function.identity()
                ));
    }

    public AccountProvider getProvider(AccountType type) {
        AccountProvider provider = providers.get(type);
        if (provider == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown account type: " + type);
        }
        return provider;
    }

    public Set<AccountType> supportedTypes() {
        return Collections.unmodifiableSet(providers.keySet());
    }
}
```

### 8. The Service (the only entry point callers use)

```java
@Service
public class AccountService {

    private final AccountProviderRegistry registry;

    public AccountService(AccountProviderRegistry registry) {
        this.registry = registry;
    }

    /**
     * Primary lookup – caller knows the type.
     */
    public Account getAccount(Integer id, AccountType type) {
        Account account = registry.getProvider(type).findById(id);
        if (account == null) {
            throw new EntityNotFoundException(
                "No " + type + " account found with id " + id);
        }
        return account;
    }

    /**
     * Optional: if the caller truly only has the id and no type,
     * you can fan-out across all providers (use sparingly).
     */
    public Account findByAnyType(Integer id) {
        for (AccountType type : registry.supportedTypes()) {
            Account account = registry.getProvider(type).findById(id);
            if (account != null) {
                return account;
            }
        }
        throw new EntityNotFoundException("No account found with id " + id);
    }
}
```

### 9. Controller / Caller

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/accounts")
public class AccountController {

    private final AccountService accountService;

    public AccountController(AccountService accountService) {
        this.accountService = accountService;
    }

    @GetMapping("/{type}/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<Account> get(@PathVariable AccountType type,
                                       @PathVariable Integer id) {
        Account account = accountService.getAccount(id, type);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(account);
    }
}
```

## Adding a New Type in the Future (e.g. `AdminAccount`)

You only **add** files. You never modify existing ones:

| Step | New file | Change to existing code? |
|------|----------|--------------------------|
| 1 | `AdminAccount.java` (entity, implements `Account`) | ❌ |
| 2 | `AdminAccountRepository.java` (extends `AccountRepository<AdminAccount>`) | ❌ |
| 3 | `AdminAccountProvider.java` (implements `AccountProvider`) | ❌ |
| 4 | Add `ADMIN` to `AccountType` enum | ⚠️ one-line enum addition |

That's it. The registry picks up the new `@Component` automatically through Spring's `List<AccountProvider>` injection. No `if/else`, no `switch`, no factory modification.

## Why Not Other Patterns?

| Pattern | Why it's worse here |
|---------|---------------------|
| **Inheritance / JPA Single-Table or Joined Inheritance** | Forces one table hierarchy; you explicitly want separate tables. Also couples entities. |
| **Simple Factory with `switch(type)`** | Violates Open/Closed – every new type requires editing the factory. |
| **Template Method** | Overkill; the "algorithm" is just one `findById` call. |
| **Strategy *without* Registry** | You'd still need a `switch` or `if` chain somewhere to pick the strategy. The Registry removes that. |

The **Strategy + Registry** combo gives you polymorphic dispatch with zero branching logic in your service, and Spring's DI makes the registry self-populating.

