### 2. The Server (thin wrapper around detection + your solver)

```cpp
// my_physics_server_3d.h
#ifndef MY_PHYSICS_SERVER_3D_H
#define MY_PHYSICS_SERVER_3D_H

#include <servers/physics_server_3d.h>
#include "my_solver.h"

class MyPhysicsServer3D : public PhysicsServer3D {
    GDCLASS(MyPhysicsServer3D, PhysicsServer3D);

    // You inherit ALL detection from the base (broadphase, narrowphase,
    // shape registration, etc.).  You only override the step.
public:
    // Override the per-space step
    virtual void space_set_physics_process(
        RID p_space, bool p_enable) override;

    // The main entry: called once per physics tick
    virtual void space_step(RID p_space, float p_step) override;

    // --- Everything else (shape registration, body creation, queries)
    //   is inherited from PhysicsServer3D / GodotPhysicsServer3D.
    //   If you need to tweak detection, override those too, but
    //   the question says keep detection as-is.
};

#endif
```

```cpp
// my_physics_server_3d.cpp
#include "my_physics_server_3d.h"
#include <servers/physics_server_3d.h>

void MyPhysicsServer3D::space_step(RID p_space, float p_step) {
    // 1. Get the space state (holds all bodies, manifolds, etc.)
    SpaceState3D *space = get_space_state(p_space); // or however you cache it

    // 2. Let the BASE class do:
    //      - velocity integration (gravity, linear/angular forces)
    //      - broadphase + narrowphase  →  fills contact manifolds
    //    Godot's base implementation does this internally.
    //    If you truly want to keep Godot's detection, you can call
    //    the parent's step but *skip* its solver.  In practice the
    //    cleanest approach is to replicate the detection calls:
    //
    //    a) Integrate velocities
    //    b) Run broadphase (BVH / SAP / etc.)
    //    c) Run narrowphase  →  ContactManifold list
    //
    //    For brevity below, assume you have:
    //      Vector<ContactManifold*> manifolds;   // from narrowphase
    //      Vector<BodyState3D*>    bodies;       // active bodies

    // 3. YOUR SOLVER
    MySolver solver;
    solver.set_iterations(8);          // solver sub-iterations
    solver.set_baumgarte(0.2f);        // position correction factor
    solver.set_slop(0.005f);           // penetration tolerance (m)
    solver.resolve(manifolds, bodies, p_step);

    // 4. Integrate positions from the new velocities
    for (BodyState3D *b : bodies) {
        b->position += b->velocity * p_step;
        // integrate angular velocity → orientation (quaternion)
        b->orientation = integrate_quaternion(b->orientation,
                                              b->angular_velocity, p_step);
    }
}
```

### 3. The Solver (the actual resolution)

This is the core. A **Sequential Impulse Solver (SIS)** with Coulomb friction and restitution:

```cpp
// my_solver.h
#ifndef MY_SOLVER_H
#define MY_SOLVER_H

#include <math_funcs.h>   // Vector3, Quaternion from Godot
#include <vector>

struct ContactPoint {
    Vector3 normal;          // from body A to body B
    Vector3 position;        // world-space contact point
    float   restitution;     // 0..1
    float   friction;        // combined μ
    // Accumulated impulses (for warm-starting)
    float   normal_impulse;
    Vector3 tangent_impulse;
};

struct ContactManifold {
    int body_a, body_b;     // indices into the body array
    Vector<ContactPoint> points;
};

struct BodyState3D {
    Vector3 position;
    Quaternion orientation;
    Vector3 velocity;
    Vector3 angular_velocity;
    float   mass;
    float   inv_mass;
    float   inv_inertia_diag[3];  // body-space diagonal (simplified)
    float   inv_inertia_offdiag[3];
    bool    is_static;
};

class MySolver {
    int   iterations_ = 8;
    float baumgarte_  = 0.2f;
    float slop_       = 0.005f;

public:
    void set_iterations(int n) { iterations_ = n; }
    void set_baumgarte(float b) { baumgarte_ = b; }
    void set_slop(float s)      { slop_ = s; }

    void resolve(std::vector<ContactManifold*> &manifolds,
                 std::vector<BodyState3D*> &bodies,
                 float dt);
};

#endif
```

```cpp
// my_solver.cpp
#include "my_solver.h"
#include <cmath>

// ---------- helpers ----------
static Vector3 cross(const Vector3 &a, const Vector3 &b) {
    return Vector3(
        a.y * b.z - a.z * b.y,
        a.z * b.x - a.x * b.z,
        a.x * b.y - a.y * b.x);
}

// Inverse inertia tensor applied to a vector (body-space → world-space)
static Vector3 inv_inertia_x(const BodyState3D &b, const Vector3 &v) {
    // Simplified: assume principal-axis inertia, rotate v into body space,
    // scale, rotate back.  For a full tensor use a 3×3 matrix.
    Vector3 v_body = b.orientation.conjugate() * v;
    Vector3 r_body(v_body.x / b.inv_inertia_diag[0],
                   v_body.y / b.inv_inertia_diag[1],
                   v_body.z / b.inv_inertia_diag[2]);
    return b.orientation * r_body;
}

// ---------- solver ----------
void MySolver::resolve(std::vector<ContactManifold*> &manifolds,
                       std::vector<BodyState3D*> &bodies,
                       float dt) {

    // Pre-compute effective mass for each contact point
    for (auto *m : manifolds) {
        BodyState3D *A = bodies[m->body_a];
        BodyState3D *B = bodies[m->body_b];
        for (auto &cp : m->points) {
            Vector3 rA = cp.position - A->position;
            Vector3 rB = cp.position - B->position;

            // K = 1/mA + 1/mB + (rA×n)·Ia⁻¹(rA×n) + (rB×n)·Ib⁻¹(rB×n)
            Vector3 ra_cross_n = cross(rA, cp.normal);
            Vector3 rb_cross_n = cross(rB, cp.normal);

            float k = A->inv_mass + B->inv_mass;
            k += ra_cross_n.dot(inv_inertia_x(A, ra_cross_n));
            k += rb_cross_n.dot(inv_inertia_x(B, rb_cross_n));

            cp.effective_mass_normal = 1.0f / (k + 1e-8f);

            // (You'd compute tangent effective masses similarly,
            //  one per tangent axis.)
        }
    }

    // ---- Sequential Impulse iterations ----
    for (int iter = 0; iter < iterations_; ++iter) {
        for (auto *m : manifolds) {
            BodyState3D *A = bodies[m->body_a];
            BodyState3D *B = bodies[m->body_b];

            for (auto &cp : m->points) {
                Vector3 rA = cp.position - A->position;
                Vector3 rB = cp.position - B->position;

                // Relative velocity at contact
                Vector3 vA = A->velocity + cross(A->angular_velocity, rA);
                Vector3 vB = B->velocity + cross(B->angular_velocity, rB);
                Vector3 rel_vel = vB - vA;

                // --- Normal impulse (with restitution) ---
                float vel_n = rel_vel.dot(cp.normal);

                // Restitution: only if approaching fast enough
                float bias = 0.0f;
                if (vel_n < -1.0f) {           // threshold to avoid jitter
                    bias = -(1.0f + cp.restitution) * vel_n;
                }

                float jn = -cp.effective_mass_normal * (vel_n + bias);

                // Accumulate (clamp to ≥ 0 for a "push apart" contact)
                float new_jn = cp.normal_impulse + jn;
                if (new_jn < 0.0f) new_jn = 0.0f;
                jn = new_jn - cp.normal_impulse;
                cp.normal_impulse = new_jn;

                Vector3 impulse = cp.normal * jn;
                apply_impulse(A, -impulse, rA);
                apply_impulse(B,  impulse, rB);

                // --- Friction (Coulomb) ---
                Vector3 rel_vel_t = rel_vel - cp.normal * rel_vel.dot(cp.normal);
                if (rel_vel_t.length_squared() > 1e-8f) {
                    Vector3 tangent = rel_vel_t.normalized();
                    float vt = rel_vel.dot(tangent);
                    float jt = -vt * cp.effective_mass_tangent;

                    float max_friction = cp.friction * cp.normal_impulse;
                    float new_jt = cp.tangent_impulse.dot(tangent) + jt;
                    new_jt = CLAMP(new_jt, -max_friction, max_friction);
                    jt = new_jt - cp.tangent_impulse.dot(tangent);
                    cp.tangent_impulse += tangent * jt;

                    Vector3 f_impulse = tangent * jt;
                    apply_impulse(A, -f_impulse, rA);
                    apply_impulse(B,  f_impulse, rB);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // ---- Position correction (Baumgarte / split-impulse) ----
    for (auto *m : manifolds) {
        BodyState3D *A = bodies[m->body_a];
        BodyState3D *B = bodies[m->body_b];
        for (auto &cp : m->points) {
            // Penetration depth (stored during narrowphase)
            float pen = cp.penetration;
            if (pen > slop_) {
                float corr = baumgarte_ * (pen - slop_) /
                             (A->inv_mass + B->inv_mass + 1e-8f);
                Vector3 correction = cp.normal * corr;
                A->position -= correction * A->inv_mass;
                B->position += correction * B->inv_mass;
            }
        }
    }
}

// Apply a world-space impulse at a point offset from the body's COM
static void apply_impulse(BodyState3D *b, const Vector3 &impulse,
                          const Vector3 &r) {
    if (b->is_static) return;
    b->velocity         += impulse * b->inv_mass;
    b->angular_velocity += cross(r, impulse);  // simplified; full tensor:
    // b->angular_velocity += inv_inertia_x(*b, cross(r, impulse));
}
```

> **Note:** I've added a few fields to `ContactPoint` (`effective_mass_normal`, `effective_mass_tangent`, `penetration`) that you'd populate during narrowphase. In real Godot these live in `PhysicsServer3D::ContactMonitor` / the manifold data.

### 4. Key Details to Get Right

| Topic | What to watch |
|---|---|
| **Warm-starting** | Store `normal_impulse` / `tangent_impulse` across frames (keyed by body-pair + contact index) to avoid jitter. Godot does this in `ContactManifold::get_points()`. |
| **Island / sleep** | Before solving, partition contacts into islands. Skip sleeping islands entirely. Godot does this in `PhysicsServer3D::SpaceState3D`. |
| **Inertia tensor** | The simplified `inv_inertia_x` above assumes principal axes. For arbitrary boxes/capsules you need the full 3×3 inverse, rotated to world space each frame. |
| **Multiple contact points** | A box-on-box manifold can have up to 4 points. You must solve them **simultaneously** (iterate all points in the same manifold within one sub-step) to avoid "tumbling" artifacts. |
| **Restitution threshold** | Don't apply bounce for slow contacts (`|v_n| < 1 m/s` or so) or you get infinite micro-bouncing. |
| **Position correction** | Baumgarte (`bias = β/dt * pen`) can add energy. Split-impulse (separate velocity and position corrections) is more stable. Godot 4 uses a hybrid. |
| **Static bodies** | `inv_mass = 0`, `inv_inertia = 0`. Skip impulse application. |

### 5. How to Get the Manifolds from Godot's Detection

If you want to **keep Godot's detection** and only replace resolution, you have two practical options:

**Option A – Subclass and re-implement `step`:**
Copy the detection portion of `GodotPhysicsServer3D::SpaceState3D::step()` (broadphase query → narrowphase → fill `ContactManifold`), then call *your* solver instead of the built-in one. This is the most "correct" but means you're maintaining a fork of the detection path.

**Option B – GDExtension / signal hook (lighter):**
If you only need to *post-process* (e.g., custom friction, custom restitution, character-controller resolution), you can:

1. Let Godot's full pipeline run (detection + its solver).
2. In your `_physics_process` or a custom `PhysicsServer3D` override of `space_step`, **after** the base call, iterate `PhysicsDirectSpaceState3D::get_direct_body_state()` for each body, read the contact info via `PhysicsServer3D::body_get_direct_state()`, and apply your own corrective impulses/positions.

This is hacky but avoids forking the detection code.

### 6. Build & Load

```bash
# Build as a Godot module (Godot 4)
scons target=editor module_my_physics_enabled=yes
```

Or as a **GDExtension** (no recompile of Godot):

```cpp
// godot_init.cpp
#include <gdextension_interface.h>

extern "C" {
void initialize_my_physics_module(void *p_level) {
    // Register your PhysicsServer3D as the singleton
}
void deinitialize_my_physics_module(void *p_level) {
    // Clean up
}
}
```

```ini
# my_physics.gdextension
[configuration]
entry_symbol = "my_physics_gdextension_init"

[libraries]
linux.x86_64 = "res://bin/libmy_physics.x86_64.so"
windows.x86_64 = "res://bin/my_physics.x86_64.dll"
```

