For the i-th element (1-indexed), the search range is [i, n-k+i] where n is the total length and k is the subsequence length. This is because:
- We need i-1 elements before, so the i-th is at position >= i.
- We need k-i elements after, so the i-th is at position <= n - (k-i) = n-k+i.

But this assumes we haven't made any specific choices yet. The key insight is: if we pick the leftmost occurrence of the minimum in the range [i, n-k+i], then for the next element, the range becomes [i+1, n-k+i+1]. This is because:
- We've used position p (where i <= p <= n-k+i) for the i-th element.
- For the (i+1)-th element, we need position >= p+1 >= i+1.
- We need k-i-1 elements after, so position <= n - (k-i-1) = n-k+i+1.

So the range for the (i+1)-th element is [p+1, n-k+i+1]. Since p >= i, we have p+1 >= i+1. And n-k+i+1 is the upper bound.

Now, if we pick the leftmost minimum (smallest p with the minimum value), then p is as small as possible, so p+1 is as small as possible, giving the largest possible range for the next element. This is optimal.

But the range for the next element is [p+1, n-k+i+1], not [i+1, n-k+i+1]. The lower bound is p+1, not i+1.

However, the standard greedy still works because:
- The minimum in [i, n-k+i] is the same as the minimum in [i, n-k+i] regardless of what we pick.
- If we pick the leftmost occurrence of the minimum, the next range is [p+1, n-k+i+1] where p is the leftmost min.
- If we picked a later occurrence p' > p of the same minimum, the next range would be [p'+1, n-k+i+1] which is a subset of [p+1, n-k+i+1].
- So picking the leftmost is at least as good.

And the minimum in [p+1, n-k+i+1] is >= the minimum in [i+1, n-k+i+1] (since [i+1, n-k+i+1] is a superset). Wait no, [p+1, n-k+i+1] is a subset of [i+1, n-k+i+1] since p >= i. So the minimum in [p+1, n-k+i+1] >= minimum in [i+1, n-k+i+1].

Hmm, but this doesn't directly prove optimality. Let me think differently.

The standard proof: consider the optimal solution. Let the i-th element in the optimal solution be at position q. We know q is in [i, n-k+i]. The minimum in this range is at position p (leftmost). We claim p <= q (or p has the same value as q and p < q).

If the value at p < value at q, then replacing q with p in the optimal solution gives a better solution (smaller i-th element), contradiction.

If the value at p = value at q and p < q, then we can replace q with p. The remaining elements (i+1 to k) in the optimal solution are at positions > q > p, so they're still valid after the replacement. The solution is the same or better.

So the greedy is optimal.

